1795385860324550	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2r7	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nScourge is a planned verifier and findings record for security testing: an agent explores with Teapot and records leads, and `scourge verify` re-runs the test itself with fresh canaries and controls. No code exists yet. The plan (notes/plan.md, revision 5) binds itself to \"measure first\" (decision D13): baseline runs and the custody spike happen before the domain catalog freezes.\n\n# Goal\nStack inventory, a development fixture both oracles can run on, a hermetic evaluation harness, baseline runs with plain tools, the custody spike, Investigation conformance, and frozen contracts.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] A written failure taxonomy from the baseline (M0.4).\n- [ ] The v1 obligation list (plan §5.4) and fixture catalog (§14) justified against that taxonomy: each kept, cut or added.\n- [ ] A decision against the rule fixed in M0.3 before any baseline run (plan §12.1, \"The M0 decision\").\n\n# Why it matters\nThe 51 invariants and 53 fixtures in the plan are provisional until this milestone's evidence exists. The premise that agents with Teapot make false authorization claims is untested for black-box work (plan §15.1). M0 is where that gets measured.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12, §17; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3, §4.\n\nAt manual close, link the taxonomy, the preregistered threshold and its decision ADR, the custody results, and the passing M0.6 conformance run with its Investigation version. Record any resulting scope change in M0.7.\n"}	blake3:Zu6WasiANPA-yONvOsjXnDFhCIcydpL2YoFxa1_WmpA
1795385860541702	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2e6	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe thinnest complete path through Scourge: record a lead, verify it with the first oracle (`web.private-object-read.v1`) on the development fixture in shared custody, and read a computed status, through the porcelain commands.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] A cold agent completes lead to verified through the harness, in both cold-start cells (M1.9).\n- [ ] The public, login-page and same-user decoys do not verify.\n- [ ] Interface failures found by the trial are fixed before M2 starts.\n\n# Notes\n- The tracker gates M1 on manual M0 acceptance, including Investigation conformance and the contract freeze. M1.4 is the first package that integrates the shipped evidence store.\n- The fixture factory (M1.10) and engagement check/draft (M1.11) run beside M1 and M2 and are not on this exit. They live under their own goal.\n- M0's decision rule may re-scope M1 before the verifier is built (plan §12.1). M0.7.5 re-cuts these bones against the frozen contracts.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.2.\n\nAt manual close, cite the M1.9 results for both cold starts and the outcomes of the interface fixes.\n"}	blake3:7wsS2MNJVRLx5OtnzMpsLNhBugmWelzl2m6QDHHpHUY
1795385860810062	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-i1o	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nCompletes the HTTP authorization product: second oracle, fix verification, crash matrix, isolated custody tier, reports and offline-checkable bundles, hardening, and the Tier 1 evaluation.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] The end-to-end scenario of plan §1.5 runs on real binaries.\n- [ ] The Tier 1 evaluation is reported with numerators and denominators (M2.10).\n\nThis is the first useful product and may ship as an HTTP-scoped preview. At manual close, link the real-binary acceptance record and Tier 1 report. Record the M3 investment decision separately against the preregistered C/B unsupported-submission and C/A supported-yield comparisons at matched budget (evaluation §7 and §11). An inconclusive comparison leaves the benefit unestablished.\n\n# Notes\nM2.0 revisits the package boundaries after the M1.9 cold-agent trial. Keep existing children when their acceptance criteria still hold.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §1.5, §12; notes/plan.evaluation.md §6, §7.\n"}	blake3:6CNnV_g8231opIcnbUx4BoKEHRnjJb7IRhf60WJvtdM
1795385860943044	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1km	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nVerification without discovery is a tool that reports nothing. M3 adds aids to exploration (surface map, coverage, next-action ranking, out-of-band collector, browser adapter, parallel workers, stateful recipes). It does not add control over exploration: the two-speed rule (D2) stands.\n\n# Entry condition\nApply the decision rule frozen before Tier 1 (notes/plan.evaluation.md §7, §11): unsupported submissions in C against B under one rubric, and supported yield in C against A, at matched budget. A tie at zero, an imprecise difference, or no supported yield in either discovery arm leaves the benefit unestablished. In that case find out why before adding breadth.\n\n# Gate\nUnassisted discovery is not worse than condition A by more than the preregistered margin, every new oracle passes its fixture and mutation gates, and the red-team track finds no promotion through the new observers.\n\n# Notes\nWritten at low resolution on purpose. M3.1 re-cuts the packages from M2 results.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §1.\n\nAt manual close, link the M3.8 results and the decision against the preregistered margin. Identify any unqualified cells.\n"}	blake3:rngZVCV9tXoAUhnBjbBdd1Ocs7sy2Wh--QuT-PIBA_w
1795385861078445	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3j7	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSIP and media verification. Sippycup authorizes from its own approval window and counts calls in its own ledger; it has no Hallpass integration. Scourge binds the Sippycup approval by reference and digest, uses `sippycup assert` as the observer where it can state the violation, and imports evidence packs with lineage. No competing journal and no debit on Sippycup's behalf.\n\n# Gate\nOne complete live signaling and media experiment on a new multi-tenant target with the real runner, capture and cancellation, plus the fixtures of notes/plan.later.md §2.5 and a mutation suite for whatever oracle ships.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §2.\n\nAt manual close, cite the M4.3 result on the named build, the fixture outcomes and the mutation results. Record any unsupported configuration.\n"}	blake3:6_Xsw18a7GVYjQyTI3F0aTbr64Do-6ZTrW7pUk7QQPA
1795385861182035	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-109	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nHeld-out Tier 2 evaluation on the frozen candidate, the support matrix, documentation, backup and restore, migrations, packaging, and claims limited to what was measured.\n\n# Exit\nThe gates of notes/plan.evaluation.md §7 are met on the frozen candidate, or the support claim is narrowed to the cells that passed. Do not lower a soundness gate to meet a date.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §6, §7, §11.\n\nAt manual close, reconcile the candidate fingerprint across the Tier 2 results, package, support matrix, user documentation, backup/restore and migration evidence. Record the supported cells and the measured basis for the release claims.\n"}	blake3:hDKv46Eb-ngJ9UGvo2gMsdIrCosqXQ3h2Pi9SgrVRpQ
1795385861325473	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1zv	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nTwo packages the plan numbers M1.10 and M1.11 run beside M1 and M2. Neither is on M1's exit, and both must be done before the Tier 1 evaluation (M2.10) starts. They sit under their own goal so that a milestone-level blocker does not hold them back or hold M1's exit open.\n\n# Goal\n- M1.10: 10 to 12 packaged Tier 1 fixtures, built by an owner other than the implementing agent, with separate access.\n- M1.11: `scourge engagement check` and `scourge engagement draft`, so writing the engagement file is cheap.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.2 (rows M1.10, M1.11 and the note under the table).\n\nExit when the independent fixture inventory, authoring-cost ledger and operator setup checks are available to M2.10. This lane can finish independently of the M1 sign-off.\n"}	blake3:gK9bOundVl6CgHdxP2n1Eq9YrkrNdYa-LhpIbu1DJxc
1795385861497238	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2el	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEverything a read says about a lead comes from one pure function over the on-chain records that name it. There is no stored `verified` flag and no promotion transaction. It works in three steps: judge each record on its own (`broken`, `stale`, `stands`), pick the status record S (the latest true `verify` record) and the deciding record D, and read the answer off them. Records are ordered by chain sequence, never by a timestamp.\n\n# Do\n`Status.compute(lead, snapshot, audience) -> status, applicability, S, D`, exactly as plan §5.1:\n- status: `integrity_failure`, `lead` (with sub-state and last outcome), `refuted`, `needs_review`, `disputed`, `conditional`, `verified`. Integrity checks come first.\n- the rule that applies to S is the admitted rule in force at its run, or the first rule admitted for that resource afterwards. So a `conditional` finding becomes `verified` with no new run.\n- applicability from D: `as_observed`, `still_violated`, `fixed_within_tested_scope`, `unestablished`.\n- re-evaluate the oracle over retained originals; a mismatch or a missing original is `broken`.\n- the audience rule: for `third_party`, only records acquired in isolated custody qualify. M1 needs the function to take the parameter; the third-party cases are exercised in M2.5.\n- the retraction branch exists only where the shipped Investigation offers the query.\nA memo by record, oracle and rule digests is a cache and never an input.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Deterministic examples identify S, D, status and applicability. The sibling M1.6.6 bone owns the generated-history regression suite.\n- [ ] F-04: one saved true observation gives `verified`, `conditional`, `conditional`, `refuted` under the four policy situations.\n- [ ] F-21 revoked oracle version is `needs_review`. F-22: class or slot change is `needs_review`; text change changes nothing; rule admission gives `verified` with no new run.\n- [ ] A verdict that disagrees with the retained originals and a missing required original are integrity failures (F-23, F-24). A changed memo cannot change the computed answer.\n- [ ] No table or file stores status or applicability.\n\nUse the latest qualifying true record for the audience. An invalid run cannot become D. A missing original for either S or D fails integrity, and a newer true record supersedes the previous baseline.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.6, §11.3; INV-02, INV-04 to INV-06, INV-50; F-04, F-21 to F-24.\n"}	blake3:SexegJrU-enqjyh3_9JlFbYsdB99bVSoBxZlGHGM26Y
1795385861511622	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2el	{"field":"size","value":"m"}	blake3:aoAR1Z9g7eU04BlKorA3KxoGkm43mSgIZpNkwF2rK0c
1795385862356242	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2s7	{"description":"Generate small offline histories of immutable records, policy changes and evidence availability. Check the public status calculation against the expected record identities and outcomes in plan §5.1. Keep the failing seed and minimized history.\n\nAcceptance\n- Ordering follows chain sequence even when timestamps disagree. Inserting an invalid run, changing a cached answer or editing only the lead text preserves the result.\n- Check both S and D for missing originals. A new true record supersedes an older retest. A later deployment change or permissive replacement rule limits present applicability while preserving history (F-22, F-43, F-52, F-53).\n- The workspace and third-party projections of the same mixed-custody history reproduce F-50. Synthetic retest records exercise the pure function in M1. The packaged-report check belongs to M2.5.\n\nRun the properties in the ordinary test suite. See plan §13, INV-02, INV-04, INV-50 and INV-51.","kind":"task","labels":["m1","test"],"parent":"bn-24s","size":"s","title":"M1.6.6 Property-test status and applicability across record histories"}	blake3:frbQI2aoMb3CRjA1v2QX2My2GyY0A4pRugpaed5K778
1795385862439305	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2s7	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-2el"}	blake3:h_IUltOAP_YE4-7wzAEjTquLdajIxczyp4nxqQPEi1s
1795385862515545	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-25j	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA read copies the rows it needs and the store revision inside one short read transaction, releases it, and only then reads evidence and re-evaluates oracles. A reader held open across a slow export would stop the write-ahead log from being checkpointed.\n\n# Do\n- Every read names its snapshot: store revision, chain checkpoint, oracle registry version.\n- A `current` label requires that the stores and registry still match after collection, checked with fresh reads, with a bounded number of retries. Otherwise the read presents the last complete named snapshot as history, or says no coherent read could be assembled.\n- The workspace read lists every standing true record with its tier, so a later, weaker run does not hide an earlier, stronger one (INV-50).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-43 (second half): an objection committed while a slow read is collecting evidence either appears, or the read names the older snapshot. It is never presented as the newer state without the objection.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.1, §10.3; INV-02, INV-42, INV-50; F-43.\n\nName the evidence-store checkpoint alongside the coordination revision, chain head and registry version. Exercise independent changes to each store and the registry during collection. Only a complete identified snapshot may be returned as historical after retries run out (plan §10.3).\n"}	blake3:BeM9m2v1-DsdueS_qY6m2rbg352HIhIhCy-leTH0x2Y
1795385862588385	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-okl	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA reproduction bundle lets a reviewer understand the claim offline and lets an authorized tester prepare a new run without the original conversation. `bundle check` runs nothing from the bundle and contacts nothing.\n\n# Do\n- `report [--kind review|reproduction|summary]`: names its snapshot, recomputes status and re-evaluates oracles (INV-04). Separate sections for verified findings, disputed or refuted findings with their records, conditional findings, findings needing review, leads, runs with unknown effects, cleanup and limits. Markdown and strict JSON.\n- The bundle: a manifest over artifacts read by ID, with the nine sections of plan §10.1. Default exports redact secrets.\n- `bundle check PATH`: three separate results: integrity, provenance and per-obligation inspectability.\n- Audience: a third-party report presents a finding as verified, or as fixed, only on records acquired in isolated custody (INV-47).\n- \"No verified findings\" never becomes \"secure\" (F-35).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.5)\n- [ ] Clean-machine checks of a complete bundle, a declared omission, a missing promised file and an altered file, then the complete bundle again with its oracle version unavailable (F-45).\n- [ ] A replaced file plus digest in an unsigned copy leaves provenance `unestablished`.\n- [ ] F-50: a mixed-custody selection for a third party presents only isolated records as verified, and a shared-custody retest leaves a finding `unestablished` there.\n- [ ] A seeded secret appears nowhere (INV-23).\n\nDepends on M1.6, M0.6 and M2.2.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §10; INV-04, INV-05, INV-23, INV-27, INV-35, INV-42, INV-47, INV-50.\n\nThe children implement the report, artifact export and recipient check, then exercise them together. M2.0 reviews this split against M1.9.\n"}	blake3:_tPRcfIfdIINDff9sGFFhjeBO7ux8RhFvZo5eEwrRc4
1795385862599143	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-okl	{"field":"kind","value":"goal"}	blake3:4j6wQJhPSrAWKUjCrwcWRwLGa7GOUUba5KnhUejl3D4
1795385862661153	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-okl	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"remove","label":"needs-split"}}	blake3:M4kQHT_0J2g1JKdojh_u0L-gO8ulOSfevTFSSTJwOeE
1795385862931125	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1vw	{"description":"Produce the Markdown and strict JSON views from one complete snapshot using the shared status function. Include record IDs, original evidence references, custody, strongest caveat, applicability, unresolved effects and cleanup.\n\nAcceptance\n- Both views identify the same snapshot, status and deciding records. A newer objection yields the newer complete view or the older named view (F-43).\n- F-35 and F-50 show the limits of the scope and audience. A missing required original for either deciding record fails integrity (F-52).\n\nSee plan §5.1 and §10.3.","kind":"task","labels":["m2","surface"],"parent":"bn-okl","size":"m","title":"M2.5.1 Render a named snapshot for the selected audience"}	blake3:Ip_WevuGP_gOe20wWr0LJ7kWNGuTBeKx4OEE817q1Hs
1795385863209097	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-bv9	{"description":"Assemble the nine manifest sections in plan §10.1 from a named snapshot and Investigation reads by ID. Preserve originals, give each derivative its own digest and lineage, and declare omitted channels and affected obligations.\n\nAcceptance\n- Resolve every promised file to its digest and preserve the exported selection's acquisition tiers.\n- Seed a secret and verify that every exported byte, path, identifier and index is clear of it. Check the original still resolves to its original digest.\n- Record the Investigation version. Exercise the shipped read interface without requiring Investigation's export API.","kind":"task","labels":["m2","evidence"],"parent":"bn-okl","size":"m","title":"M2.5.2 Export evidence by ID with a redacted manifest"}	blake3:3oSYza2_3Df_HbVpKVI7OuDhYJuIGkrKu8oOnbyAaHc
1795385863461178	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2ey	{"description":"Implement the recipient command and distinguish the three results in plan §10.2. Use recipient-owned trust settings and locally available oracle versions.\n\nAcceptance\n- Cover a complete bundle, declared omission, missing promised file, altered file and unavailable oracle (F-45). An unsigned copy with a replaced file and matching edited digest has unestablished provenance.\n- A disagreement on re-evaluation fails integrity. Report an obligation as uncheckable when its original is omitted or its evaluator unavailable. Show the historical status at its named snapshot.\n- The command executes no bundled code and sends no traffic.","kind":"task","labels":["m2","evidence"],"parent":"bn-okl","size":"m","title":"M2.5.3 Check bundle integrity, provenance and inspectability offline"}	blake3:HO64YoC-8xPoFFqgUviNg6cnBOA4_Z7FnA_qckBL0i8
1795385863753905	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1td	{"description":"Use the producing CLI and shipped evidence store to create the third-party report and bundle. Give a reviewer the export and its instructions on a clean offline machine with the checker. Record producer and recipient versions.\n\nAcceptance\n- Reconcile record IDs, snapshot, custody labels and evidence links across Markdown, JSON and the recipient result. Repeat with an omitted channel and unavailable oracle. Confirm the recipient can locate the bounded claim, caveat, original evidence or declared omission, cleanup and residual effects without coaching.\n- Put the checked recipient commands and their result meanings in the user guide. Feed any navigation failures into the report changes before close. The separate maintainer-decision pilot stays in M2.10.\n\nSee plan §10 and evaluation §13–14.","kind":"task","labels":["m2","integration","docs"],"parent":"bn-okl","size":"s","title":"M2.5.4 Check an exported report and bundle on a clean recipient machine"}	blake3:mqsgfu24OMSHTPVWzBgjmi67uzOG6j_NzPKvbp2gycQ
