1795469533663649	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-73w	{"body":"Started in workspace bn-73w"}	blake3:_68OakH--Vdm35nSQHkyg0WvJ6MueYXLBnLAMbeqqRo
1795470358731719	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2e6	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe thinnest complete path through Scourge: record a lead, verify it with the first oracle (`web.private-object-read.v1`) on the development fixture in shared custody, and read a computed status, through the porcelain commands.\n\n# Exit\n- [ ] A cold agent completes lead to verified through the harness, in both cold-start cells (M1.9).\n- [ ] The public, login-page and same-user decoys do not verify.\n- [ ] Interface failures found by the trial are fixed before M2 starts.\n\n# Notes\n- The tracker gates M1 on manual M0 acceptance, including Investigation conformance and the contract freeze. M1.4 is the first package that integrates the shipped evidence store.\n- The fixture factory (M1.10) and engagement check/draft (M1.11) run beside M1 and M2 and are not on this exit. They live under their own goal.\n- M0's decision rule may re-scope M1 before the verifier is built (plan §12.1). M0.7.5 re-cuts these bones against the frozen contracts.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.2.\n\nAt manual close, cite the M1.9 results for both cold starts and the outcomes of the interface fixes.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] The M1 exit consumes the frozen schema, cross-lane, Teapot argv, and oracle registries named in `docs/adr/m1-contract-recut.md`; M1.10/M1.11 remain independent and are not exit dependencies.\n- [ ] The M0 additions are exercised only in their routed packages; no M2-only oracle is treated as an M1 deliverable.\n"}	blake3:hli4fqGikdYFZHUoCVRCroFB6Lzg3NqgtBdSxQSmLxI
1795470358790189	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-264	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe `scourge-core` crate: domain types, the coordination store and request keys. Everything else in M1 builds on it.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.1)\n- [ ] Lost-response, key-reuse and revision-conflict tests.\n- [ ] A schema test listing the allowed tables.\n\nDepends on the contract freeze (M0.7).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4, §8.6, §9.1; INV-12, INV-13, INV-25, INV-29.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Core values use the exact version tags and canonical encodings registered by `scourge.contract-registry.v1`, `scourge.engagement.v1`, `scourge.policy-rule.v1`, and `scourge.claim-template.v1`.\n"}	blake3:M6gmwgwdRnoZsbWV7iFb2NyPS-O2ncpFhs-wLzF0y8k
1795470358840453	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-xwe	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nFive nouns for agents (engagement, lead, experiment, run, finding) and the internal objects of plan §4.2. IDs are opaque with a readable prefix (`L-`, `R-`, `V-`) and are never derived from titles, URLs or time.\n\n# Do\n- Types for lead and lead revision, lead event, run and step, experiment, verification record index, and the result dimensions of §5.2 as separate enums (run state, external effects, evidence completeness, oracle result, reproduction, cleanup, custody, target isolation, enforcement, policy source, applicability).\n- Canonical JSON with SHA-256, using Investigation's rules. Reject duplicate keys wherever a manifest, experiment or record is read (INV-44).\n- Secrets never appear in identifiers or summaries (INV-23): credentials are referenced by the instrument's session name.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Serialization round-trip tests, and a duplicate-key document is rejected.\n- [ ] Two executions always get two run IDs, even when every byte matches.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.1 to §4.3, §5.2; INV-23, INV-44.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Canonical JSON and typed IDs cover every exact shape/version in `scourge.contract-registry.v1`; digests are over the registered canonical bytes and unknown identifiers are not guessed.\n"}	blake3:3HiMIReWzh99sPbCeWtOAMz5I3rXkLikXO_W7LaS9-4
1795470358894392	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2ij	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nAgents never invent a request key. Each effectful command mints one, persists it with the run before any external effect, and prints it. This is what makes a lost response recoverable without repeating an action.\n\n# Do\n- Persist the run row and request key before any external call (INV-12).\n- Reusing a key with the same input returns the original run and never starts another. With different input it returns `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED` (INV-13).\n- An unknown key and another engagement's key get the same answer (INV-25).\n- Revision conflicts on lead edits return `REVISION_CONFLICT`.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Lost-response test: the command is killed after the run row is written, and the same key reopens the same run.\n- [ ] Key-reuse tests for same input and different input.\n- [ ] Revision-conflict test.\n- [ ] A crash test at the first boundary shows nothing external happened before the run row existed.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.5, §8.6; INV-12, INV-13, INV-25.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Normalize command plus typed arguments as `scourge.request-input.v1` before selector resolution; request-key equality is exact equality of that same-run normalized object.\n"}	blake3:xFfl47sonhmCrmYbRRXolYhrnVjaAW85AMo-4JIMtKg
1795470358946533	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1wp	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe engagement says what is authorized: the pass, the principals, the declared properties with their policy rules, and the limits. It references the pass and does not restate what the pass binds.\n\n# Do\n- Parse and validate the engagement file against the schema frozen in M0.7.1.\n- Store each revision as an immutable manifest with its digest and the pinned pass digest. Amending the file creates a new revision.\n- Policy rules: audience (`owner`, `group`, `authenticated`, `public`) for reads and writes, with source. A rule counts only when the operator admitted it. An unset rule admits nothing.\n- A changed, expired or revoked pass stops new runs (INV-45).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The development fixture's example engagement loads; malformed files fail with a usage error that names the stanza.\n- [ ] `Policy.allows(rule, exposure, principals)` is a pure function with tests for all four audiences and both exposures.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.5, §4.6, §11.3; INV-06, INV-45.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Parse and validate exactly `scourge.engagement.v1` plus referenced `scourge.policy-rule.v1`; operations, resources, selectors, sessions, pass/project and target version remain inert registered references.\n- [ ] Missing policy rules or `target_version` leaves a draft partial/non-final; do not accept raw request grammar in an engagement.\n"}	blake3:PC3IBAKVQcI5BUObu3fQjQcR4OqH1QVKUMQ-Xab6_zo
1795470359003341	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2c6	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe agent-facing surface for reads. \"Obvious to an agent with zero knowledge\" is a set of measured gates: bare `scourge` fits in 2 KiB, and a useful result arrives within three calls.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.2)\n- [ ] Size tests.\n- [ ] Machine-mode error tests.\n- [ ] Every suggested argv is launched in a new process with `FORMAT` unset, from an unrelated directory with spaces in the path and two engagements, and the reply parses as one JSON object.\n- [ ] The exit-code table is generated and complete.\n- [ ] Nested hostile config invokes nothing.\n\nDepends on M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §8; INV-15, INV-21, INV-30 to INV-35, INV-46; F-28.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Every machine response implements the required fields and nullability of `scourge.response.v1`; every failure code maps to the exact exit in `scourge.contract-registry.v1`.\n"}	blake3:226QmqgqSXIG3k7-oQLCvvAEE1Mja2myfrbGpaoxNBw
1795470359064187	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-dip	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA reviewer routinely runs the CLI inside someone else's source tree. A `scourge.toml` found by walking up from the current directory selects a workspace and grants nothing. Executable paths, the evidence root, credential sources and sockets come only from an operator binding written by `scourge init` outside any inspected tree (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/scourge/`), keyed by workspace identity, root path and configuration digest.\n\n# Do\n- `scourge init PATH --engagement FILE` creates the workspace and the binding. The binding file is written atomically (temporary file, flush, rename, directory flush).\n- Without a valid binding the CLI shows its embedded orientation and the binding status, and invokes nothing.\n- `--workspace` or the nearest `scourge.toml` locates a workspace. With more than one engagement, case-specific commands need `--engagement`. No implicit \"last\" object for mutations (INV-46).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-28: a nested `scourge.toml` naming a sentinel adapter and a symlinked evidence root causes no adapter invocation, host probe, secret resolution or foreign read.\n- [ ] Selection tests with two engagements.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §8.7, §9.1; INV-21, INV-46; F-28.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Workspace/binding failures use the registered `scourge.response.v1` envelope and exact exit-3 codes; init validates the frozen engagement schema without inventing request grammar.\n"}	blake3:5TKWUrGZJWIpUkc1RHTJHWGyjd-9ynGLr_Je5ei7-YU
1795470359118138	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1aw	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nFollow the stack's conventions: `--format text|pretty|json`, a hidden `--json` alias, the `FORMAT` variable, and a JSON object envelope with an `advice` array. Agents branch on `result` and `error.code`, not on the exit code.\n\n# Do\n- The `scourge.response.v1` envelope as frozen in M0.7.2.\n- In machine mode every outcome is one JSON object on stdout, including usage and parse errors raised before a workspace is known. Unavailable identifiers are `null`. No prompts, pager, browser or logs on stdout (INV-32).\n- An error says what failed, whether anything was committed, whether an external effect may have occurred, and the next valid step as an argv with its effects.\n- One enumeration of error codes generates the exit-code table (exit 0, 2 to 8). A read that reports a blocker exits 0 with the code in its result.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Machine-mode tests for a parse error, an unknown workspace and a refusal: each is one JSON object.\n- [ ] A test asserts every error code has a row in the generated table, and `--json` equals `--format json`.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.4, §8.5; INV-32.\n\nDiagnostics go to stderr as structured, redacted events keyed by workspace, engagement, request key and run (plan §11.4), never to stdout in machine mode. A test runs a refusal with a seeded secret and checks that stdout is one JSON object and that the stderr events carry the keys and not the secret.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Generate `scourge.response.v1` and the complete frozen error/exit table, including `SLOT_CONFLICT`, `ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE`, `METHOD_REFUSED`, and `ACCOUNTING_UNESTABLISHED`.\n- [ ] M0-ADD-01: wrong-listener/tool-unavailable diagnostics remain typed with native and wrapper exits even when an outer wrapper exits zero.\n"}	blake3:AsLcgMD1G_DRH7bik9kVkcLEypHOfUooXSE-kSg0Gc4
1795470359188141	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-24v	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nBare `scourge` is read-only, works anywhere and fits in 2 KiB. Inside a bound workspace it shows state: the engagement, counts, the Teapot findings and leads not yet recorded here, the principals, properties and classes a lead needs, and one next action. `tldr`, `status`, `brief` and `onboard` are aliases.\n\n# Do\n- The entry screen of plan §8.1, with omission counts and drill-down routes when something does not fit.\n- A mandatory caveat is never dropped to fit the budget: shorten or withhold the claim (INV-35).\n- A run with unknown effects, pending cleanup or a broken chain takes the `Next` line ahead of everything else.\n- A lint over catalog strings: Scourge's own objects are named only with the five nouns, never with a synonym from the fixed list (candidate, hypothesis, issue, attempt, case, verdict, conclusion), and an instrument's object is written \"Teapot finding\" or \"Teapot lead\" (INV-33).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Size tests: bare `scourge` and default `status` each fit in 2 KiB at 0, 10 and 10,000 leads (INV-30).\n- [ ] Truncation tests keep the caveat.\n- [ ] The lint runs in CI and fails on a synonym.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §1.4, §8.1; INV-30, INV-33, INV-35.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Entry/status machine output is `scourge.response.v1`; the operator-bound transport and at least one supported action are discoverable without target traffic.\n- [ ] M0-ADD-01: the suggested argv is directly runnable and transport/tool masking remains typed.\n"}	blake3:PstslfqC7IYZU-7Z14wYZtFndkiVy1aWrQxY3IZm4H0
1795470359233623	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-r3b	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`show ID [--depth title|summary|full]` works for leads, findings, runs, records and instrument flow IDs. `list [leads|findings|runs]` is the bounded listing the entry screen's omission counts point to.\n\n# Do\n- Implement both as short read transactions.\n- Target content in any output is delimited as untrusted and size-bounded. This is hygiene, not a boundary. Raw evidence is opt-in.\n- A network-sink test over the whole read-only surface: status, help, `show`, `list`, `next`, `--plan`, `doctor` (INV-15).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] `show` and `list` tests on a seeded store.\n- [ ] The network-sink test fails if any read-only command opens a connection to the target.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.3, §8.4; INV-15, INV-22.\n\nImplement the read-only doctor view in this slice. On a seeded store it displays each recorded diagnostic and its age, identifies missing results, and leaves both stores untouched (plan §9.4). Run the network-sink assertion against the commands shipped at this point, and extend it as each later read command lands.\n\nThe network sink also fails on any outbound connection from these commands, not only one to the target. Local reads, status and reports need no telemetry, upload, model provider or Internet access (plan §11.4).\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] `show`/`list` machine output is `scourge.response.v1`, including complete finding/run fields, blockers, diagnostics, advice, next actions, and transport; reads remain traffic-free.\n"}	blake3:y0mtdi9c9zxXYn1plAujPMUZJYecvV_cgdcmN6hYp40
1795470359279233	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-23h	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEvery `next` entry is a complete argv that runs from the caller's directory. It carries the workspace and engagement whenever resolution depends on them, and in machine mode it names the format, because a new process with no `FORMAT` set would answer in text (INV-31).\n\n# Do\nA test harness that collects every suggested argv the CLI prints, then launches each exactly as printed: in a new process, with `FORMAT` unset, from an unrelated directory whose path contains spaces, in a workspace with two engagements.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every reply parses as one JSON object.\n- [ ] The harness fails if a command prints a suggestion it did not exercise.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.4; INV-31.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Launch every `scourge.response.v1.transport.action.argv` from an unrelated directory with spaces, multiple engagements and `FORMAT` unset; parse exactly one response envelope.\n"}	blake3:HOo6uD6PF3AqIPbEBjAmIOJy0jejAfaaN-3PGZwPB3E
1795470359339087	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1st	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe verifier reaches the target only through the instrument. The adapter implements the nine operations of the instrument contract with the argv pinned in M0.1.3. Structured invocation only: resolved executable, argv array, cleaned environment, JSON in and out. No shell strings, and no parsing of prose for IDs or outcomes.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.3)\n- [ ] Contract tests against the real binary for every operation, including engine mismatch and a refused unenforceable pass.\n- [ ] The edit surface is read, and a run is refused while anything in it could change a verifier send.\n\nDepends on M0.1 and M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2 to §7.4; INV-16, INV-17, INV-49.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] The adapter is generated from `scourge.teapot-argv.v1`; captured and schema-only variants remain distinguished and no flag, position, result key, or capability is guessed.\n"}	blake3:UiQ2y5ToswE7u_5Kg_GqWmPx_PYXCM5tu8HIu33a248
1795470359476697	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-20h	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe read-only half of the instrument contract.\n\n# Do\n- A subprocess layer: resolved executable from the operator binding, argv array, cleaned environment, JSON parsed strictly. Native exit codes are retained separately.\n- Describe: version, engine identity, enforcement state, protocol limits. The engine that answers must be the one recorded.\n- Check session: logged in, logged out or unknown, per principal.\n- Dry run: request count and whether it is within the ceiling.\n- Units left, and list jobs for a window.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Contract tests against the real pinned Teapot for each operation.\n- [ ] An engine-mismatch test: the adapter refuses when the answering engine is not the recorded one.\n- [ ] A missing binary is `DEPENDENCY_UNAVAILABLE`, never a pass.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Implement exact `scourge.teapot-argv.v1` argv, result keys, schemas and optional arguments for describe/check-session/dry-run/units/jobs.\n- [ ] M0-ADD-01: preserve native and wrapper diagnostics for unavailable tools and wrong listeners.\n"}	blake3:LDBUmsdJVxXN5ID6vh9Q0j94Deef2pA1zrgXOyZUCqI
1795470359618344	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3s5	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe effectful half. Every recipe flow is one request sent under a named session, then read back by flow ID as original bytes.\n\n# Do\n- Send one request under a named session, with the per-send reference M0.1.3 found (or the gap recorded).\n- Run a declared macro for a multi-step plant.\n- Read a flow by ID: original request and response bytes, timing, destination.\n- A login or retry the instrument adds to a step is recorded as a flow of that step.\n- Cancel means request, then observe.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Contract tests against the real binary on the development fixture for each operation.\n- [ ] A send with capture off or lost returns \"no flow\", and the adapter does not report that as \"not sent\" (INV-41).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.2, §7.5; INV-41.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Implement the exact send, macro-send/replay, stored-flow-read and cancel variants in `scourge.teapot-argv.v1`; schema-only variants stay explicitly qualified until captured.\n"}	blake3:9NNqPfOznRTHmkYzOMkDm6EarHWxRASRBbpucEoCdwk
1795470359733803	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-g2i	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nRules, intercepts, script hooks and session behaviors in the instrument could change a verifier send or the response stored for it. The verifier reads that edit surface at run start and run end and keeps it in the record. Enforcement is a separate label: each run records `egress_enforced` or `unenforced` as the instrument reports it.\n\n# Do\n- Read the edit surface. If anything could change a verifier send or its stored response, refuse with `INSTRUMENT_EDITS_ACTIVE`, unless the instrument offers a send mode that bypasses them and records that it did.\n- When the engagement requires enforcement and the instrument cannot enforce the pass, refuse with `ENFORCEMENT_UNAVAILABLE`. No flag or retry downgrades it (INV-17).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] With an active match-and-replace rule on the verifier's path, the run is refused before any send.\n- [ ] F-26: enforcement required and unavailable is refused, with no downgrade offered.\n- [ ] A test for Teapot's `pass-not-enforceable` refusal.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.4, §7.2, §7.3; INV-17, INV-49; F-26, F-49.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Derive edit-surface/enforcement capability only from exact `scourge.teapot-argv.v1` operations and outputs; unavailable capabilities produce the registered refusal/dependency codes, never guessed behavior.\n"}	blake3:DaCcZG0VP_croR--4ZoqBPevIzF1wE2qyOGyAsULYk8
1795470359804050	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-14o	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe instruments own accounting (D9). Scourge never debits a budget. Teapot draws units in blocks and never refunds, its counter includes spending from outside Teapot, and a block drawn in one run can pay for a later one. So a change in the counter is not one run's charge.\n\n# Do\nPer run, capture: pass ID and digest, instrument job IDs, the dry-run ceiling, the counters before and after (each may be absent), where each came from, its granularity and the window. `attribution` is `exclusive` only with positive evidence covering every spender, `shared` when another spender was seen, and `unestablished` otherwise, which is the usual value.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-44: two saved counter observations with the same job list and the same change, one including an outside spender; an absent counter; a block that pays for two jobs. All give `unestablished` and no per-run charge.\n- [ ] An absent counter stays unknown and is never reported as zero.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.4; INV-16; F-13, F-44.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Store the exact nullable fields of `scourge.accounting.v1`; every count carries unit/source/granularity/window/evidence and attribution remains `unestablished` absent positive coverage.\n"}	blake3:wGE7sAe2xNjxQh5O1pxK59lEqIx2YrCPS4pjqmiiJdc
1795470359866683	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1qi	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nScourge holds no evidence blobs. Originals go to the evidence store, which is a slice of Investigation, through the three-operation contract (capture, read, record) qualified in M0.6.\n\n# Do\nImplement the `Evidence` interface on Investigation's recording crate, or its JSON protocol: open an evidence run, attach original flow bytes with digests, finalize; read by ID with digest and lineage; record derived artifacts (engagement manifest, experiment, verification record). Record the Investigation version every run used. A build with no retraction branch refuses to run on an Investigation version that offers retraction, so a retraction is never silently ignored.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.4)\n- [ ] Originals are stored and read back by ID with matching digests.\n- [ ] A retry returns the same artifact.\n- [ ] Originals are immutable, and a redaction creates a derivative with its own digest and lineage (INV-27).\n\n# No claim\nA labeled in-memory double is allowed in unit tests only. It never satisfies this bone.\n\nDepends on M0.6 and M1.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2; INV-27, INV-29.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Consume the named passing M0.6 Investigation version/commit and its public machine contract; do not begin M1.4 from an unqualified or unnamed Investigation build.\n"}	blake3:3m7BDM_e2vhOTE2UBsAY1v_Ps-DLNZ_o85WJahA3NmU
1795470359926741	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2w2	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe core of the product. `scourge-verify` is a separate binary and the only code that mints canaries, sends recipe flows, evaluates oracles and appends verification records. The first oracle is `web.private-object-read.v1`: the verifier creates its own objects with fresh canaries and reads them as owner, other user and anonymous. HTTP status is not an input.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.5)\n- [ ] Every listed fixture passes: F-01 to F-11, F-14, F-36 to F-39, F-42.\n- [ ] The mutation suite kills every listed check.\n\nDepends on M0.2, M1.3 and M1.4.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.5, §6.1 to §6.5, §6.7; INV-01, INV-03, INV-07 to INV-09, INV-20, INV-36, INV-37, INV-40.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] The package implements `web.private-object-read.v1` exactly as frozen in `scourge.oracle-specifications.v1`, including POR-01..POR-12 and the out-of-class dispatch boundary.\n"}	blake3:Y3i7RggCffs9FAC6biXRklJfUf80oM-fxiYjSLSb6lI
1795470359996778	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-tnk	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA claim template belongs to a pack and is compiled into the verifier. Agents do not author controls. Slot values from a lead are untrusted. The slots are `owner`, `other`, `read`, `plant` and `remove`. A lead that names a declared property takes `read`, `plant` and `remove` from it and supplies the principal pair.\n\n`plant` and `remove` run with the owner's credentials. A plant request written by the agent can create the object already shared with `other`, and then every control passes on a false claim.\n\n# Do\n- `Templates.resolve(lead, engagement)` returns an experiment or the missing slots. The record says who supplied each slot.\n- `--slot` cannot override a slot the property fixes (`SLOT_CONFLICT`).\n- A `read` request supplied by a lead must use GET or HEAD, or the run is refused with `METHOD_REFUSED` and nothing is sent (INV-37).\n- An experiment whose owner-side requests came from a lead is marked so status can cap it at `conditional` (INV-36).\n- Freeze the experiment with a digest that binds template version, resolved slots, principal bindings, oracle version and limits, and not the canary.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Unit tests on `Templates.resolve`: a lead-supplied plant marks the experiment so that status caps it at `conditional`; a slot override is `SLOT_CONFLICT`; a DELETE or POST bound to the read slot is `METHOD_REFUSED`.\n- [ ] The experiment digest changes with each bound field and does not change with the canary.\n\nThe end-to-end halves are elsewhere: F-38 and F-39 run through the real verifier with a network sink in M1.5.4, and `--plan` announcing the cap is checked in M1.6.4. This bone needs no adapter, so it can start as soon as M1.1 is done (plan §12.3 lanes).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.2; INV-36, INV-37; F-38, F-39.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Validate and instantiate exact `scourge.claim-template.v1` using ordered registered operation IDs. Property operation slots are operator-only; leads may select a declared property and principal pair but cannot supply or replace operations.\n- [ ] A supplied value for a fixed/operator-only slot fails with `SLOT_CONFLICT` before traffic.\n"}	blake3:0F8Rk2Lem_PqDEzXyH5dmEQQ335brx9n6biNhPPTbQw
1795470360047837	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-10s	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA verify run follows ten steps (plan §6.1): persist the run row; take the run lock for the pass and the conflict groups for the principals' sessions; check engagement revision, pass and instrument; freeze and store the experiment; open an evidence run and record the accounting counters and the highest flow ID; send each round's flows; evaluate the oracle over those flows only; clean up; publish the record; finalize.\n\n`scourge-verify` is a separate binary with no model call. It is the only code that mints canaries, sends recipe flows, evaluates oracles and appends verification records. It takes no observation or result from a caller (INV-01, INV-03).\n\n# Children\n- M1.5.2.1 Preflight, locks and the frozen experiment (steps 1 to 4).\n- M1.5.2.2 Rounds: canaries, step intents, sends, flow read-back and identity probes (steps 5 and 6).\n- M1.5.2.3 Cleanup of objects the run created (step 8).\n- M1.5.2.4 Oracle evaluation, error mapping and the stored record (step 7, and step 9 up to a stored record).\n\nPublication onto the per-engagement chain (the write-ahead intent and the head advance of step 9) is M1.6.2. Until it lands, a stored record is on no chain and no status is computed from it.\n\n# Exit\nThe children are done, and one complete run on the development fixture with the cross-user read switched on goes through every step except chain publication.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.1, §7.5 to §7.7; INV-01, INV-03, INV-18, INV-20, INV-40; F-14, F-27.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Execute the exact required flow order and predicate registered for `web.private-object-read.v1`; store the resulting exact `scourge.verification.v1` shape.\n"}	blake3:Hym63yIoGn9f8eC4L1eINEOgSbfllqetIcysoDEtqLk
1795470360117627	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1wj	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSteps 5 and 6 of a verify run (plan §6.1). Every recipe flow is one request sent under a named session and read back by flow ID as original bytes.\n\n# Do\n- Open an evidence run. Record the accounting counters as the instrument reports them (M1.3.4) and the highest flow ID before the first send.\n- Mint canaries: 128 random bits as 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters, one per declared field per object per round, never reused across rounds or runs (INV-20).\n- Persist each step's intent (step number, request digest, flow-ID floor, send reference) before sending it, and its outcome after.\n- Send each flow through the adapter (M1.3.2). Read every flow back by ID and store the original bytes through the evidence bridge with digests.\n- Run the identity probes at the start and end of every round.\n- Record a login or retry the instrument adds to a step as a flow of that step.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A complete round on the development fixture with the cross-user read switched on: every flow is stored and read back by ID with matching digests.\n- [ ] A unit test on canary minting: length, alphabet, and no repeat across rounds or runs in a large sample.\n- [ ] Killing the verifier after a step intent is persisted and before its send leaves the intent visible to recovery (M1.7).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.1, §6.5 (\"Canaries and matching\"), §7.2, §7.4, §7.5; INV-20, INV-41.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Rounds implement the frozen I1/I2/P/P2/F0/F1/F2/F3/F4/I3/I4/C flow contract, per-field canaries, original-byte retention, and registered completeness/reason codes.\n"}	blake3:beXwGyAkGhR7d1k-HpUu0lKKwOfu6EACGvqKwp-HC6o
1795470360182615	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2a3	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nSteps 1 to 4 of a verify run (plan §6.1). Nothing reaches the target until every check here has passed, and a refusal says which check failed.\n\n# Do\n- Persist the run row and its request key before any external effect (M1.1.3, INV-12).\n- Take the run lock for the pass and the conflict groups for the principals' sessions and mutable resources. Runs against one pass are serialized (INV-18).\n- Check the engagement revision, the pinned pass digest and the instrument's identity: describe, check session, and a dry run within the ceiling (M1.3.1).\n- Read the instrument's edit surface and enforcement state, and refuse with `INSTRUMENT_EDITS_ACTIVE` or `ENFORCEMENT_UNAVAILABLE` (M1.3.3, INV-17, INV-49).\n- Freeze the experiment from `Templates.resolve` with its digest (template version, resolved slots, principal bindings, oracle version and limits, not the canary) and store it through the evidence bridge.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Two concurrent runs on one pass do not overlap, and neither do two runs that share a principal's session.\n- [ ] Each failed preflight check refuses with its own code, and a network sink sees no target traffic.\n- [ ] The stored experiment reads back by ID with the digest the run row names.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.1, §6.2, §7.2, §7.3; INV-12, INV-17, INV-18, INV-49.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Freeze template/property/slots/profile/oracle and experiment digest using the registered versioned shapes before target effects; preflight refuses incomplete or unavailable dependencies with registered codes.\n"}	blake3:tgTMvNvRaWHgdfBnmjnDLzhjge9C-6ZiM21Jls2gigs
1795470360254412	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-27k	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe verifier tests only with objects it created in the run, so removing them cannot destroy anything that existed before. Cleanup removes an object only when the plant's own response established that this run created it (INV-40).\n\n# Do\n- Remove each object whose creation by this run is established. When creation is uncertain, delete nothing and record cleanup as owed.\n- Record the cleanup outcome per run (INV-19). Owed cleanup shows on the entry screen's `Next` line (M1.2.3).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-27: a failed cleanup is recorded and the finding may still verify; a lost plant response deletes nothing and leaves cleanup owed; an object with the same name that existed before the run survives byte for byte.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.5, §7.7; INV-19, INV-40; F-27.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Cleanup follows POR-11: attempt removal only when the run's plant response establishes ownership, and retain the exact cleanup outcome in `scourge.verification.v1`.\n"}	blake3:5dO9b3RKQnN4CsKAN620rj8JatSyrsPUSRg_R6v4ilU
1795470360304029	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3lg	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nStep 7 of a verify run, and step 9 up to a stored record. The oracle judges only flows the verifier itself sent in this run (INV-03).\n\n# Do\n- Pass the oracle (M1.5.3) the typed observations of this run's flows and nothing else. The oracle registry is compiled into `scourge-verify`.\n- A crash in the instrument or the oracle is `error`, never `false` and never `true` (F-14).\n- Build the verification record with the fields of plan §6.9, including the known misses that apply, and store it through the evidence bridge.\n- Chain publication is M1.6.2. This bone stops at a stored record that is on no chain.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] An injected instrument crash and an injected oracle panic each give `error`.\n- [ ] The stored record reads back by ID, and re-evaluating its oracle over the retained originals gives the same result.\n- [ ] No input of `scourge-verify` accepts an observation, a result or a record (INV-01).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.1, §6.4, §6.7, §6.9; INV-01, INV-03; F-14.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Persist every required field of `scourge.verification.v1`; map oracle invalid/error/incomplete results to the frozen reason and exit-code registries without promoting them.\n"}	blake3:-XkEZ6WXhAFHbMCwSH2qA8Tg9JA-p5zDwyLUq8YrqPY
1795470360356102	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-20u	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe oracle consumes the verifier's own typed observations for one round and returns `true`, `false`, `invalid` with reason codes, or `error`. Flows: identity probes I1, I2; plants P, P2 (two new objects, each with its own canaries); F0 anonymous read before the owner's; F1 owner read; F2 other-user read; F3 anonymous read; F4 other user reads the second object; identity probes I3, I4; cleanup C.\n\n# Do\n`Oracle.evaluate(observations) -> result`, pure:\n- `true`: the first object's canary is in F1 and absent from F4, the subjects are distinct and unchanged, and the canary is in F0, F2 or F3. Exposure is `unauthenticated` when it is in F0 or F3, otherwise `cross_principal`. Note `after_owner_read` when F0 lacks it and F3 has it.\n- `false`: setup valid and the canary absent from F0, F2 and F3.\n- `invalid`, never `false`: `SAME_SUBJECT`, `OBSERVER_INVALID`, `CANARY_NOT_SPECIFIC`, `SESSION_DRIFT`, `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n- Matching runs over the decoded body. Transfer and content codings are undone under a size and ratio limit and the decoder is named. A body over the limit is `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n- HTTP status is not an input (INV-08). The oracle never reads a policy rule.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Unit tests for each outcome and each reason code on saved observations.\n- [ ] The function's signature has no status-code field and no policy input.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.4, §6.5; INV-07, INV-08; F-42.\n\nThis function is pure and needs no adapter or evidence store, so it can start as soon as M1.1.1 is done (plan §12.3 lanes). M1.5.7 property-tests it over generated observations.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Implement `web.private-object-read.v1` as the pure frozen POR-01..POR-12 predicate, invalid precedence, exposure derivation, reason codes and known-miss boundary in `scourge.oracle-specifications.v1`.\n"}	blake3:eiNZAF4jvDm4foCIRfVdhx2OYGt30Zbp7a6fcaJqvtA
1795470360399868	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-36n	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEach fixture pairs the right behavior with a tempting wrong conclusion. They are runnable cases under `fixtures/oracle/` and regression tests from here on.\n\n# Do\nImplement as end-to-end cases against the development fixture, through the real verifier and adapter:\nF-01 same subject under two labels; F-02 public object, with and without a rule; F-03 200 login page; F-05 403 carrying the canary; F-06 stale cache; F-07 session drift; F-08 canary echo; F-09 a canary or flow from an earlier run; F-10 agent-supplied flow IDs are ignored; F-11 missing or truncated flow; F-14 instrument or oracle crash; F-36 owner-only property with no authentication check; F-37 shared cache after the owner's read; F-38, F-39 (resolved in M1.5.1, run here end to end with a network sink); F-42 compressed and escaped canary, and an oversized body. F-11 and F-42 use the fixture modes of M1.5.6.\nF-04 needs the status function and is finished in M1.6.3.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every listed case runs in CI against the real fixture and passes with the result plan §14 requires.\n- [ ] No case passes by being skipped: a missing dependency fails the suite.\n\n# No claim\nPassing fixtures shows the oracle handles these cases. Whether each check is doing work is the mutation suite's job (M1.5.5).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §14.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Cover POR-01..POR-12 using the frozen fixture mappings. M0-ADD-03: F-08 collection disclosure and F-03 object existence/status remain separately scoped leads; neither is a negative or promotion under object-read.\n"}	blake3:WNfWzfL6inc8PNMO_Sj3t2P1g6HJTPG6UE22rgmbiPg
1795470360458616	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-26a	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe oracle specification lists its checks: each identity comparison, each control, each canary correlation, each completeness test. The mutation suite removes one at a time and runs the fixture catalog. This is the strongest soundness evidence the project can produce cheaply, because it is mechanical and repeatable. An oracle version is registered only when it passes.\n\n# Do\nBuild the mutation mechanism (a switch per listed check that disables it) and a runner that reports, per mutant, which fixtures failed.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every listed check of `web.private-object-read.v1` is killed by at least one fixture: 100%.\n- [ ] A surviving mutant is resolved either way: a fixture is added, or the check is removed as doing nothing.\n- [ ] A mutant that removes the status-code independence (makes status an input) is killed by F-03 or F-05.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.7; notes/plan.evaluation.md §9; INV-08, INV-09.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Mutation coverage kills each POR-01..POR-12 check and preserves the exact unsupported-class boundary from `scourge.oracle-specifications.v1`.\n"}	blake3:_sXcGnDpWoStu0Gf1MoaFou2lCaeM1aKV7HJWMsio98
1795470360541044	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-26c	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nTwo first-oracle fixtures need response shapes the development fixture (M0.2) does not serve. F-42 needs a canary in a compressed body, a canary inside an escaped JSON string, and a body over the decoding limit. F-11 needs a truncated response. Without these modes, M1.5.4 would have to skip these cases or fake them, and a skipped case is not a pass.\n\n# Do\nOn the orders read route, add administration-only modes, each switchable per request:\n- gzip and deflate content coding, and chunked transfer coding;\n- the note returned inside an escaped JSON string (a JSON document embedded as a string field);\n- a body larger than the verifier's decoding limit, and a small compressed body that expands past the ratio limit;\n- a response that closes the connection partway through the body.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A test per mode shows the bytes on the wire with plain HTTP requests.\n- [ ] Each mode is in the ground-truth file with the result F-11 or F-42 requires.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.5 (\"Canaries and matching\"), §14 (F-11, F-42).\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Response modes exercise POR-08/POR-09 completeness and bounded decoding while retaining original bytes; truncation/undecodability yields `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n"}	blake3:yTS8xMN2EccBvYXrmkvPa1ZQuy7JPbc8fn2SxVVw-8M
1795470360593412	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-jcq	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`Oracle.evaluate` is pure (plan §11.3), so its specification can be checked over generated observations as well as over the hand-written fixtures. The mutation suite (M1.5.5) shows that every listed check does work on the catalog. Properties show that the function behaves as specified on inputs nobody wrote by hand. This bone does for the oracle what M1.6.6 does for the status function.\n\n# Do\nGenerate rounds of typed observations (identity probes, plants, F0 to F4, a canary placed or absent in each flow, bodies in each supported coding) and check:\n- changing only HTTP status codes and reason phrases never changes the result (INV-08);\n- the result is `true` only when every listed check of the frozen specification holds, and breaking any single check never gives `true`;\n- a failed setup check gives `invalid` with that check's reason code, never `false` (INV-07);\n- the result does not depend on which supported coding carries the canary, and a body over the limit gives `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`;\n- the exposure and the `after_owner_read` note follow from F0, F2 and F3 exactly as §6.5 says.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The properties run in the ordinary test suite with a fixed case budget, and a failing seed is kept with its minimized round.\n- [ ] A deliberately broken oracle that reads the status code fails the first property.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.5, §11.3; INV-07, INV-08.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Generated observations satisfy the exact frozen predicate, invalid precedence, exposure derivation, POR-01..POR-12, and unsupported-class boundary.\n"}	blake3:15eGKx3G0QEacKLha4SrfHRt7BRlMdmKW84bSZ2U3WU
1795470360634225	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-20l	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEvery shipped oracle publishes its flows, its invalid reasons and its known misses (D10), in the form of plan §6.5 (§6.7, \"Documentation\"). The first oracle ships in M1, so its guide ships with it and not at M5. A guide written by hand beside the code drifts, so its tables are checked against the code.\n\n# Do\n- Write `docs/oracles/web.private-object-read.v1.md`: the flows with principal, action and what each rules out; the predicate for `true`, `false` and `invalid`; every reason code; the known misses; and the policy rules that decide each exposure.\n- Generate the reason-code table and the numbered check list from the oracle's own enumerations, or test that the guide lists exactly those.\n- `verify --plan`, and `show` on a record, link to the guide and name the known misses that apply (record field in §6.9).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A test fails when a reason code or listed check is in the oracle and not in the guide, or the reverse.\n- [ ] The guide's examples run against the demo fixture (INV-34).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md D10, §6.5, §6.7, §6.9; INV-34.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] The checked guide names the exact shipped class and POR-01..POR-12. M0-ADD-03: collection disclosure and object existence/status are separately scoped classes, not object-read negatives.\n"}	blake3:ZbaF1Gy2ZosgYvlO1ddpt32AgPs0RnOiZO-GKJ74sGM
1795470360687726	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-24s	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nJoins the verifier to the surface: recording leads, running `verify`, the hash-chained verification record, and status as a pure function computed on every read.\n\n# Acceptance evidence (plan §12.2, row M1.6)\n- [ ] Status property tests, including a changed cache and a missing original, and retraction where the shipped Investigation offers it.\n- [ ] No command accepts a verdict.\n- [ ] A Teapot finding at `certain`, and one marked `fixed`, change no status and no applicability.\n- [ ] The workspace read lists every standing true record with its tier, and each audience's status record is the latest true record that qualifies for it.\n- [ ] An objection committed during a slow read cannot vanish from a report that claims the newer state.\n- [ ] Two concurrent reconciliations leave one record and one head.\n\nDepends on M1.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §5, §6.9, §9.3; INV-02, INV-04 to INV-06, INV-28, INV-39, INV-42, INV-48, INV-50.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] The package writes exact `scourge.verification.v1` and `scourge.publication-intent.v1` records and exposes frozen response/error semantics, including `ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE` before traffic.\n"}	blake3:6vwdZ1EOZhwo5TMUuPOQeLxUlcLHSs_SkeSZTi5Nak0
1795470360731801	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3gx	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA lead is anything unverified and costs one call. It needs only text, or only `--from`. Flow and Teapot references are hints, never evidence.\n\n# Do\n- `lead add TEXT [--flow ID] [--from teapot:finding:N|teapot:lead:N] [--class C] [--property P] [--slot k=v] [--policy TEXT]`.\n- Given `--flow` and no class: read that flow's session name and route from the instrument, match them against the declared properties, and put a filled-in `verify --plan` in `next`.\n- Given `--from`: take the title, endpoint and evidence flow IDs from Teapot and map the check to a class where the pack declares one. Copy nothing from a response body. Send no traffic.\n- `lead set`, `lead withdraw`, `lead defer`, `lead duplicate` as revisions and attributed, append-only events. Material fields are class and slots.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A text-only lead is created in one call.\n- [ ] An imported Teapot finding becomes a lead with origin `teapot`, and its confidence is shown as Teapot's claim.\n- [ ] A network sink shows `lead add` sends nothing to the target.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §4.1, §8.3.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Lead input may select declared class/property and principal slots but cannot supply operator-only property operations; fixed-slot overrides fail `SLOT_CONFLICT` before traffic.\n"}	blake3:A-XmN44Dv90NF27mLuQb1qdhYCB0bjQ1faJcswM6HO4
1795470360777610	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2f7	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nOne immutable record per run, with the fields of plan §6.9. Each engagement has its own chain: a record contains the digest of the one before it. Publication is write-ahead so that recovery never puts a record on the chain that the verifier did not write.\n\n# Do\n- Before the verifier stores a record it persists a publication intent: engagement, run, record digest and the head it expects.\n- Then, in one transaction: link the record once for that run, check the head is still the expected one, advance it, mark the intent complete.\n- If the head has moved, nothing is linked, and the verifier rebuilds the record on the new head under a new intent.\n- `scourge run reconcile` finishes only a publication with a matching intent. An import cannot create an intent.\n- A record-shaped artifact with no intent is not on the chain and is ignored by status.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-20: an imported record-shaped artifact and a lookalike offered to `run reconcile` are never linked; one genuine pending publication reconciled twice at once yields one record and one head.\n- [ ] A broken chain is `INTEGRITY_FAILURE`.\n\n# No claim\nIn shared custody the chain detects accidents and casual edits. It does not stop a determined same-account forger, and reports say that.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.9, §9.3; INV-01, INV-28; F-20.\n\nThe record this bone publishes is the one M1.5.2.4 stored. Until this bone lands, a stored record is on no chain.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Store exact `scourge.verification.v1`, checkpoint and `scourge.publication-intent.v1` shapes; digest canonical bytes and reconcile only a matching intent/run/predecessor without target traffic.\n"}	blake3:ppJxXp3HYWp01uq2LIccSsylEGO1hgBGvDBCCUXs2ak
1795470360825274	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2el	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEverything a read says about a lead comes from one pure function over the on-chain records that name it. There is no stored `verified` flag and no promotion transaction. It works in three steps: judge each record on its own (`broken`, `stale`, `stands`), pick the status record S (the latest true `verify` record) and the deciding record D, and read the answer off them. Records are ordered by chain sequence, never by a timestamp.\n\n# Do\n`Status.compute(lead, snapshot, audience) -> status, applicability, S, D`, exactly as plan §5.1:\n- status: `integrity_failure`, `lead` (with sub-state and last outcome), `refuted`, `needs_review`, `disputed`, `conditional`, `verified`. Integrity checks come first.\n- the rule that applies to S is the admitted rule in force at its run, or the first rule admitted for that resource afterwards. So a `conditional` finding becomes `verified` with no new run.\n- applicability from D: `as_observed`, `still_violated`, `fixed_within_tested_scope`, `unestablished`.\n- re-evaluate the oracle over retained originals; a mismatch or a missing original is `broken`.\n- the audience rule: for `third_party`, only records acquired in isolated custody qualify. M1 needs the function to take the parameter; the third-party cases are exercised in M2.5.\n- the retraction branch exists only where the shipped Investigation offers the query.\nA memo by record, oracle and rule digests is a cache and never an input.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Deterministic examples identify S, D, status and applicability. The sibling M1.6.6 bone owns the generated-history regression suite.\n- [ ] F-04: one saved true observation gives `verified`, `conditional`, `conditional`, `refuted` under the four policy situations.\n- [ ] F-21 revoked oracle version is `needs_review`. F-22: class or slot change is `needs_review`; text change changes nothing; rule admission gives `verified` with no new run.\n- [ ] Re-evaluation that detects a changed verdict or memo fails with INTEGRITY_FAILURE (F-23). A missing required original also fails integrity (F-24).\n- [ ] No table or file stores status or applicability.\n\nUse the latest qualifying true record for the audience. An invalid run cannot become D. A missing original for either S or D fails integrity, and a newer true record supersedes the previous baseline.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.6, §11.3; INV-02, INV-04 to INV-06, INV-50; F-04, F-21 to F-24.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Status/applicability reads exact frozen record fields and enums; invalid, unsupported or unlinked artifacts never promote a lead.\n"}	blake3:4q5T0JMzEGWRfLLMvJO9Q71fMwv06L-8GPWF3SDPtaM
1795470360876221	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-23g	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`verify` and `retest` are the only porcelain commands that send target traffic. Each says what it will do, and its `--plan` form shows it first and sends nothing.\n\n# Do\n- `verify LEAD --plan`: the matched template, the resolved slots and who supplied each, any unfilled slot as a form, the flows and their count, the effects, the request ceiling from the dry run, the custody, target isolation and enforcement the run would have, and the highest status the result can reach with the policy rule as it stands. For a class with no template: `ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE`, the supported classes, and that the lead stays a lead.\n- `verify LEAD [--rounds N]`: the ten-step run. Two rounds by default; two true rounds is `reproduced`.\n- No command, file, import or API accepts a verdict, an oracle result or a record from a caller (INV-01).\n- An instrument's own confidence, verification state, fixed state or export is never an input (INV-48).\n- Only the operator admits a policy rule; a lead can propose one (INV-39).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] End to end on the fixture: lead, `--plan`, `verify`, and `show` reads `verified` with the full badge (INV-05).\n- [ ] F-48: promoting a Teapot lead, running `teapot findings repro --verify` until it says `fixed`, and exporting Teapot's report change no Scourge status and no applicability.\n- [ ] A catalog schema test shows no command has an input for a verdict.\n- [ ] `--plan` sends nothing (network sink).\n- [ ] F-38: for a lead whose plant came from the lead, `--plan` announces that the highest status the result can reach is `conditional`.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §6.1, §8.2, §8.3; INV-01, INV-05, INV-15, INV-39, INV-48; F-48.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] M0-ADD-03: explicitly scoped collection-disclosure and object-existence/status inputs stay leads. `verify --plan` is read-only and returns `ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE` plus supported classes; `verify` returns exit 5 before target traffic.\n- [ ] F-08 and F-03 are adjudicated independently and are neither promotions nor negative object-read findings.\n"}	blake3:cpZmmr7ccHZlsOsvNRUG6ZpuiqrPbvSaVU3BDJ5rcBg
1795470360926435	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-25j	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA read copies the rows it needs and the store revision inside one short read transaction, releases it, and only then reads evidence and re-evaluates oracles. A reader held open across a slow export would stop the write-ahead log from being checkpointed.\n\n# Do\n- Every read names its snapshot: store revision, chain checkpoint, oracle registry version.\n- A `current` label requires that the stores and registry still match after collection, checked with fresh reads, with a bounded number of retries. Otherwise the read presents the last complete named snapshot as history, or says no coherent read could be assembled.\n- The workspace read lists every standing true record with its tier, so a later, weaker run does not hide an earlier, stronger one (INV-50).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-43 (second half): an objection committed while a slow read is collecting evidence either appears, or the read names the older snapshot. It is never presented as the newer state without the objection.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.1, §10.3; INV-02, INV-42, INV-50; F-43.\n\nName the evidence-store checkpoint alongside the coordination revision, chain head and registry version. Exercise independent changes to each store and the registry during collection. Only a complete identified snapshot may be returned as historical after retries run out (plan §10.3).\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Coherent snapshots include the exact deciding/status record identifiers from `scourge.response.v1`; an unsupported or unlinked record cannot appear as a promotion.\n"}	blake3:Qb2M9MqgN3poShjodEHgSiMTG3WfEvoCzFPAB5VAWXs
1795470360957778	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2s7	{"field":"description","value":"Generate small offline histories of immutable records, policy changes and evidence availability. Check the public status calculation against the expected record identities and outcomes in plan §5.1. Keep the failing seed and minimized history.\n\nAcceptance\n- Ordering follows chain sequence even when timestamps disagree. Inserting an invalid run after a true record leaves S and D in place. Editing only the lead text preserves the result. A conflicting stored verdict or memo fails integrity on re-evaluation (F-23).\n- Check both S and D for missing originals. A new true record supersedes an older retest. A later deployment change or permissive replacement rule limits present applicability while preserving history (F-22, F-43, F-52, F-53).\n- The workspace and third-party projections of the same mixed-custody history reproduce F-50. Synthetic retest records exercise the pure function in M1. The packaged-report check belongs to M2.5.\n\nRun the properties in the ordinary test suite. See plan §13, INV-02, INV-04, INV-50 and INV-51.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Property histories use exact frozen verification/publication shapes and prove invalid, unsupported and unlinked artifacts cannot affect status/applicability.\n"}	blake3:2hmrlsY1W8p8C7rDmR1gn9LjMY5gOH19PMZd1uh9j2Y
1795470361003019	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1j8	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nAn agent must never repeat a state-changing action because it did not see the result. A statement that a step was or was not sent needs evidence bound to that run and step. The instrument can send a request and keep no flow, and two callers can send the same bytes, so an idle engine, a missing flow and a matching request digest each prove nothing alone.\n\n# Do\n- `Recovery.reconcile(run)`: reconcile from the instrument's record of that send, or a recorded refusal before dispatch. Record `observed` for an established effect and `none` for an established absence. Otherwise effects stay `unknown`, conflicting runs stay blocked, and nothing is repeated automatically.\n- `run show`, `run show --request-key KEY`, `run reconcile`. None starts a new run or sends target traffic.\n- The entry screen names a run with unknown effects ahead of everything else.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.7)\n- [ ] On the fixture's act-then-drop route: effects unknown, nothing repeated.\n- [ ] F-12: a send with no retained flow, and two sends with the same digest, both stay `unknown`. A recorded refusal before dispatch establishes that nothing was sent.\n- [ ] Every lookup keeps the original run and sends nothing.\n\nDepends on M1.6.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §7.5; INV-14, INV-41; F-12.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Recover by exact `scourge.request-input.v1` equality in the same run; no automatic resend. Conflicting or unresolved effects use `CONFLICTING_RUN`/`EFFECT_UNKNOWN` with the registered exits.\n"}	blake3:hjIgqtH7lfnQOVVX-PnM48YdZfa7n4S9cgci8Z1cRRE
1795470361048798	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-24d	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`scourge demo PATH` is the tutorial on the development fixture. `init` may add one owned block to `AGENTS.md`, in Edict's owned-block style, and tracks its digest. The block is short on purpose and is not the enforcement mechanism: the binary works without it.\n\n# Do\n- `demo`: start the fixture, create a workspace, and walk the path of plan §8.2.\n- The managed block text of plan §8.9. Other blocks and user edits are untouched.\n- Generated-example tests: help advertises only what is implemented, and every printed example runs against the demo fixture (INV-34).\n- Never accept a host's trust dialog, disable harness restrictions or install global hooks (INV-43).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.8)\n- [ ] Every printed example runs.\n- [ ] No overwrite outside the owned block, tested on an `AGENTS.md` with other blocks and user edits.\n\nDepends on M1.2 and M1.6.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.9; INV-34, INV-43.\n\nUpdate the README status and command examples for the delivered M1 surface. Run the documented examples alongside the generated help checks on a fresh demo. Label future commands by milestone so readers can tell which commands are available.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Demo and managed-block machine surfaces emit exact `scourge.response.v1`/error semantics and exercise only the frozen M1 oracle class.\n"}	blake3:p2H0qZ0ig7Pgk-uWD8V_qZ_tI7eB0lvOTqgZShlzVbs
1795470361094078	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-360	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe interface has to work for an agent that has never seen it, not only for its author. A cold agent receives the executable on its PATH, the task objective, a prepared engagement with credentials, and permission to use normal tool help. No system prompt with the command sequence, no coaching, no warm-up.\n\n# Do\nThrough the harness, in both cold-start cells (executable only, and the workspace as `scourge init` leaves it with the managed block), run a handful of cold-agent sessions on the development fixture. Record what the host actually loaded into the agent's context.\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M1.9)\n- [ ] A cold agent completes lead to verified in each cell.\n- [ ] The public, login-page and same-user decoys do not verify.\n- [ ] The concept check passes: after the entry screen only, the agent states what the tool is for, what it must not do, and its next call.\n- [ ] The gates of plan §1.4 are measured: first useful result within three calls; mutating calls from lead to verdict.\n- [ ] Interface failures are fixed before M2 starts. Each fix is a bone.\n\n# No claim\nA handful of runs on the development fixture catches gross interface failures. It supports no rate.\n\nDepends on M1.6, M1.7 and M1.8.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §1.4; notes/plan.evaluation.md §3, §6 (Skeleton tier).\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] M0-ADD-01: generated transport argv works and masked transport/tool failures remain typed.\n- [ ] M0-ADD-02: preflight run-root capacity and retain interrupted/truncated nested artifacts as invalid and unsettled, never scored.\n- [ ] M0-ADD-04: a supplied F-37 lead verifies with `after_owner_read` visible; count discovery separately.\n"}	blake3:J45JX4KJ6tw03a5i6su_5muKVHDVlq9OjOktdfBpl2Y
1795470361154593	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3bh	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe engagement is the largest setup cost, and whoever writes it is often an agent too. Two operator commands keep it cheap. Neither is on the agent's path.\n\n# Children\n- M1.11.1 `engagement check`.\n- M1.11.2 `engagement draft --from-teapot`.\n- M1.11.3 The unset-rules case and the operator setup record.\n\n# Exit (plan §12.2, row M1.11)\n- [ ] F-51: `check` names a stanza with no canary field, one with no remove route and one whose plant does not establish creation, and prints its effects first.\n- [ ] A draft from the instrument's capture of the development fixture passes `check` once the operator has set its rules.\n- [ ] A true result on a property whose rules are unset is `conditional`.\n- [ ] Setup calls, time and errors are recorded.\n\nRuns beside M1 and M2. Must be done before M2.10 starts.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6; notes/plan.evaluation.md §7 (Operator setup); F-51.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Check/draft consume exact `scourge.engagement.v1`, `scourge.policy-rule.v1`, `scourge.claim-template.v1` and registered Teapot references; neither command sends target traffic except the explicitly authorized check probes.\n"}	blake3:Sny1f7TEZWfmcoqoqvFv1UJm2aCQxr4e7_JhynWSX7E
1795470361201132	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3jd	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`scourge engagement check` is an operator command, not on the agent's path. It finds engagement stanzas that cannot support an oracle before any lead exists, so the operator does not learn it from a failed verify run (plan §4.6).\n\n# Do\n- Print the effects first, in the form `verify --plan` uses. Then run each principal's identity probe, and each property's plant and remove, once.\n- Persist a run and request key before any effect (INV-12). Remove only objects whose creation the plant's response established (INV-40), with the rule from M1.5.2.3.\n- Name each stanza that cannot support an oracle: no field that can hold a canary, no remove route, or a plant whose response does not establish creation.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-51, first three cases: each unusable stanza is named, and the effects were printed before anything was sent.\n- [ ] On the development fixture's example engagement, `check` passes and leaves no object behind.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6, §6.5; INV-12, INV-40; F-51.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Validate exact engagement/policy/template schemas and inert registered references. Report unusable stanzas with frozen codes; never reinterpret raw request grammar into the manifest.\n"}	blake3:_I8evO1jxL3-74mygOHVRQfHG4fDbVEQYFKMEkZZUfw
1795470361242106	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-28q	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nWriting the engagement is the largest setup cost. `scourge engagement draft` starts it from what the instrument has already captured (plan §4.6).\n\n# Do\n- `scourge engagement draft --from-teapot [--openapi FILE]` writes one property for each resource shape in Teapot's site map and parameter inventory, and in the API schema if one is given.\n- It sends no traffic and leaves `readers` and `writers` unset.\n- It does not read Teapot's authorization matrix or findings, so the draft says nothing about where a flaw might be.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] A network sink sees nothing during `draft`.\n- [ ] With Teapot findings and an authorization matrix present, the draft is byte-identical to one made without them.\n- [ ] A draft from Teapot's capture of the development fixture passes `engagement check` once the operator has set its rules.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6; notes/plan.evaluation.md §2 (hint parity).\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Draft exact `scourge.engagement.v1` using only inert references from `scourge.teapot-argv.v1`. It remains partial/non-final with rules unset or no `target_version`, and sends no traffic.\n"}	blake3:nhdy4JVMYXnHY7fKLxjB12iccvdWD9c5o7uRjPyOso4
1795470361274126	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-iv7	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe last case of F-51, and the setup measurement that M2.10's operator-setup trial reads (notes/plan.evaluation.md §7).\n\n# Do\n- Verify a lead on a property drafted with its rules unset.\n- For an operator going from a Teapot capture of the development fixture to an engagement that passes `check`, record calls, wall time and errors in the evaluation ledger's format.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-51, fourth case: a true result on a property whose rules are unset is `conditional`.\n- [ ] The setup record exists for the development fixture, in the format M2.10 uses.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6, §5.1; notes/plan.evaluation.md §7, §11; F-51.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Validate exact `scourge.policy-rule.v1`; unset rules leave the draft partial and admit no policy. Only an operator setup record can admit a rule.\n"}	blake3:7QGtq0jFMJSPYsFQMhmhPyu982wLgg6DRqbVLNdZ4iQ
1795470361320069	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-ijh	{"field":"description","value":"Wire the existing M1 checks into the ordinary project test entry point as they land. For the retained invariants and fixtures in the M0 disposition, identify their named test or qualification runner. Keep host-dependent qualification results separate from the fast local run.\n\nAcceptance\n- On a fresh checkout, the documented command runs the shipped status properties, store tests, read-only network-sink assertions and generated example checks. A deliberately stale example or failing property makes the command fail.\n- Show which retained checks are due in a later milestone and which require a named runner. Skips and unavailable dependencies remain visible in gate results.\n\nSee plan §13–14 and evaluation §13.\n\nAlso in scope:\n- The fuzz corpora of M1.13 replay in the fast run. Longer fuzz runs are recorded separately.\n- The oracle properties of M1.5.7 and the oracle-guide check of M1.5.8 run in the fast run.\n- The instrument contract tests run against the versions pinned in the M0.1 inventory, as a named runner. A change to a pin re-runs them before the new pin is accepted, because Teapot is still moving (plan §15.1, \"Stack drift\").\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] `just check` runs all frozen schema, cross-lane, Teapot argv, oracle, and M1 reconciliation gates, including the complete 55-bone KEEP/0-CUT/0-SPLIT disposition.\n"}	blake3:qsp8OW_MMR41fQcG8ftS8fbEgU6omqtIACY44Wn_0Xo
1795470361376965	m07-recut-codex	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2ai	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nScourge parses bytes it does not control: target responses (through the body decoder), the instrument's JSON output (which carries target content), engagement files written by an operator or an agent, and canonical JSON read back from the evidence store. The hostile-input invariants (INV-22, INV-44) and the decoder's size and ratio limit (§6.5) are claims about every input, and hand-written cases cannot cover every input. A panic or an unbounded allocation on one of these paths is at best a verifier crash (`error`, F-14) and at worst a way around a check.\n\n# Do\nCoverage-guided fuzz targets, pinned with the other dependencies, for:\n- the canonical JSON reader: never panics; a document with a duplicate key is always rejected (INV-44); an accepted document re-serializes to the same canonical bytes;\n- the engagement file parser: never panics; every rejection names a stanza;\n- the adapter's parser for instrument output: never panics; malformed output is a contract error, never a pass;\n- the body decoder: bounded time and memory under the size and ratio limit; over the limit gives `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`; never panics.\nKeep a seed corpus per target, taken from the contract fixtures and the development fixture.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] The fast test suite replays every corpus as a regression, including every crash found.\n- [ ] A longer run per target is recorded outside the fast suite, with its duration, the code version and the result.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §3.3, §6.5, §8.4; INV-22, INV-44; notes/plan.evaluation.md §13.\n\n# M0.7 frozen-contract acceptance\n- [ ] Fuzz every parser for `scourge.engagement.v1`, `scourge.policy-rule.v1`, `scourge.claim-template.v1`, cross-lane shapes, Teapot argv outputs and oracle records; malformed bytes cause typed errors and no effects.\n"}	blake3:qDQFKM-nPbCDU37TEQx9ACIMMqz4BXOZn29u5GinfJY
